WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
SURPRISING POPULARITY:
Recently, I was looking at a list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:08.360
detailing the most visited websites in the US.&nbsp;
At the top, you had your common culprits. Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:16.120
YouTube, Reddit, Amazon, Facebook, DuckDuckGo…&nbsp;
wait a minute, DuckDuckGo is the 6th most visited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:22.280
website within the US pulling in 2.3 billion&nbsp;
visits every single month? What the hell? That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:28.440
means that DuckDuckGo is almost twice as popular&nbsp;
as Bing, an official search engine from Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:34.600
who has ChatGPT as a partner. This also means that&nbsp;
DuckDuckGo is also more popular than Instagram,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:40.880
Twitter, Netflix, Uber, Airbnb, Spotify, and&nbsp;
basically any other service that I didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:46.400
name at the beginning. If you’re like me,&nbsp;
this is a shocker. I mean, I’ve heard about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.920
DuckDuckGo here and there in passing as some sort&nbsp;
of private alternative to Google search. But I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:58.200
never actually seen a single person actually&nbsp;
use DuckDuckGo. Also, if I’m being honest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:03.600
most of these so-called alternatives never really&nbsp;
make it that far due to just how powerful big tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:09.640
is. Things like Signal and BeReal usually just&nbsp;
have 5 seconds of fame before fading into oblivion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:15.600
never to be heard of again. But that doesn’t&nbsp;
seem to be the case with DuckDuckGo. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:22.320
DuckDuckGo boasts a user base of 80 million people&nbsp;
and that was as of 2020. That number is likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:28.320
closer to 100 million today. Obviously, these&nbsp;
numbers are nothing in direct comparison Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:34.560
but in comparison to everything else, it’s&nbsp;
insane. But not only has DuckDuckGo grown to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:40.600
insane heights but they’ve also stood the test&nbsp;
of time. DuckDuckGo launched on February 29,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:46.000
2008 meaning that they’ve been going for nearly&nbsp;
16 years at this point, or about the same time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.640
as Bing. But unlike Bing, DuckDuckGo didn’t have&nbsp;
the full power and might of Microsoft behind them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:57.640
They started off as just a no name player and&nbsp;
have quietly risen to be one of the most popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:03.720
websites not just in the US but around the world&nbsp;
where they rank as the 10th most visited website.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:09.000
So here’s the insane story of DuckDuckGo and&nbsp;
how they were able single handedly break into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:21.342
the cutthroat search engine market and take&nbsp;
on some of the biggest companies in the world.

00:02:21.342 --> 00:02:23.880
HUMBLE BEGINNINGS:
Taking a look back, the story of DuckDuckGo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:30.320
takes us back to the village of Valley Forge,&nbsp;
Pennsylvania to a man named Gabriel Weinberg. Over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:35.400
the years, Gabriel has kept a pretty low profile&nbsp;
which is not all the surprising given the type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:40.320
business that he runs. There’s almost nothing&nbsp;
about his childhood online other than the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:46.680
that he was born on New Years Day of 1979 and he&nbsp;
attended local schools. What we can assume though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:52.560
is that Gabriel was extremely sharp as he would&nbsp;
end up attending MIT on two separate occasions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:57.040
Once for his bachelors where he majored in Physics&nbsp;
and once for his masters where he majored in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:02.200
Technology Policy. He didn’t just jump straight&nbsp;
into DuckDuckGo after college though. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:07.360
Gabriel ran multiple ventures before DuckDuckGo.&nbsp;
The first of these was an educational product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:13.000
called learnection. The goal of learnection was to&nbsp;
increase parental involvement in primary schools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:19.720
and though it was a noble cause, it didn’t really&nbsp;
go anywhere. He would end up spending 2.5 years on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:24.880
it before trying to sell it to a non profit. But&nbsp;
after those talks fell through, he would just go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:29.320
on to work at the non profit instead to save up&nbsp;
some money. Using this cash, he would go on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:35.560
create another startup called NamesDatabase in&nbsp;
2003. A rather unoriginal name but it did have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:40.760
a rather novel concept. It was basically a social&nbsp;
media site that made it easy to find people that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:44.720
you went to high school and college with. In&nbsp;
order to search on the website, you could either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:50.240
pay a membership fee or refer someone else to the&nbsp;
website which is quite a clever growth strategy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:56.440
Within just a few years, NamesDatabase would grow&nbsp;
to 20 million free users and 50,000 paying users.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:02.160
But, despite the success of NamesDatabase, Gabriel&nbsp;
was self admittedly not all that interested in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.320
product itself. So, he would end up selling a good&nbsp;
offer came around from their biggest competitor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:12.772
Classmates.com for an estimated $10 million in&nbsp;
cash in March of 2006. Most people would’ve just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.772 --> 00:04:16.080
called it quits right here. After all, having&nbsp;
$10 million by the time your 30 means that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:20.720
could just invest the money well and end up as&nbsp;
a billionaire by the time you die. But, Gabriel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:25.840
didn’t sell NamesDatabase to take things easy. He&nbsp;
sold it so that he could spend all his effort on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:31.760
something that he truly believed in and could work&nbsp;
on for decades. The only issue is that he didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:37.120
exactly know what that was, so he would just spend&nbsp;
his time working on a bunch of side projects that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:42.760
he found interesting. One of these side projects&nbsp;
was a Google search results optimizer. The tool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:47.280
would try to filter out spam and content farms&nbsp;
and prioritize results from websites with direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.320
answers to the search. Gabriel would further build&nbsp;
upon this idea over the course of an year until he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:58.120
ended up with his own search engine that he would&nbsp;
dub DuckDuckGo.com. If you’ve ever wondered what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:03.800
the name means, well then, I’m sorry to say that&nbsp;
there is no real meaning to the name. It was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:07.760
something that popped into Gabriel’s head one&nbsp;
day when he was thinking about the childrens game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:13.960
Duck, Duck, Goose and he ran with it. Clearly,&nbsp;
Gabriel isn’t all that into naming and branding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:18.960
but he did choose a rather auspicious&nbsp;
day to launch DuckDuckGo: February 29,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:29.480
2008. And with that, Gabriel had officially&nbsp;
began the uphill journey of taking on Google.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:35.080
WHY DUCKDUCKGO?:
Shortly after launch, DuckDuckGo did receive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:39.880
some notable press coverage. For example, they&nbsp;
would be featured on TechCrunch’s Elevator Pitch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:46.080
Friday in December of 2008. They would also end up&nbsp;
as a finalist on the BOSS Mashable Challenge. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:50.600
while this press coverage definitely didn’t&nbsp;
hurt, the key to DuckDuckGo’s growth has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:55.800
always just been word of mouth. People tell their&nbsp;
friends about this private alternative to Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:00.160
they give it a shot, they like it, and then&nbsp;
they tell more people about it. Hearing this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:05.840
you’re probably wondering what’s so special about&nbsp;
DuckDuckGo in the first place. Well, obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:10.840
it offers privacy but it goes much deeper than&nbsp;
just that and it’s completely different to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:16.280
something like incognito on Chrome. All incognito&nbsp;
does is is locally delete your search history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:21.760
after you close the browser. Incognito doesn’t do&nbsp;
anything to stop websites or Google from tracking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:27.320
your history. In fact, Google will usually be&nbsp;
helping these websites continue to track you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:32.080
If you didn’t know, Google is by far the&nbsp;
most popular choice for companies to track&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:37.120
website activity. You know, what’s the click&nbsp;
through rate, how long is the average session,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:42.600
what time of day are people visiting, how likely&nbsp;
are they to convert, and so much more. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:47.680
75% of the top one million websites in the&nbsp;
world use Google trackers to optimize their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:53.800
business. And Facebook trackers are present on&nbsp;
25% of the top websites. As a website owner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.800 --> 00:07:00.240
these analytics tools are a god send but as a&nbsp;
user, they’re not all that great. The good news is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:06.600
that DuckDuckGo actively tries to block as many of&nbsp;
these trackers as possible. So, using DuckDuckGo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:10.920
not only gives you more privacy when searching&nbsp;
but it makes your more invisible across the web&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:17.480
in general. Also this often makes websites a lot&nbsp;
faster as these trackers won’t be bogging down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:22.440
performance. Putting privacy and performance aside&nbsp;
though, the benefit that intrigues me the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:28.360
is actually search quality. Now, this might be a&nbsp;
bit confusing to hear. How could a small startup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:33.440
possibly have better quality search results&nbsp;
than Google? Well, for one, DuckDuckGo isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:39.200
as incentivized as Google to push their own&nbsp;
ads meaning that you’ll see less ads but more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:44.760
importantly, DuckDuckGo doesn’t try to pander to&nbsp;
your thoughts and beliefs. You see, Google’s main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:49.720
goal isn’t to deliver to you the best and most&nbsp;
accurate search results possible. It’s actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:54.640
in their best interest to show you search results&nbsp;
that best align with what you already think. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:59.960
way, you’re pleased with what you see and are&nbsp;
more likely to continue your Googling session. On&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:06.200
a broad scale, this means that if you lean left,&nbsp;
you’re likely to be fed CNN and NBC news articles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:11.800
and if you lean right, you’re likely to be fed Fox&nbsp;
and Daily Wire news articles. But this works on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:17.320
much more micro scale as well. If you think that&nbsp;
Lucids are way better than Teslas, then you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:23.080
likely to find reviews that praise Lucids even if&nbsp;
that’s the minority of reviews. DuckDuckGo on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:27.400
other hand doesn’t know anything about you meaning&nbsp;
that they couldn’t curate results based on your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:33.000
interests even if they wanted to. So, DuckDuckGo&nbsp;
basically offers a more private internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:39.280
experience with less ads, better performance, and&nbsp;
no bias. No wonder so many people who are in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:45.080
know have opted for DuckDuckGo. But, despite&nbsp;
everything that’s going for DuckDuckGo, taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:51.320
on Google is obviously still no easy task, which&nbsp;
brings us into the growing pains of DuckDuckGo.

00:08:51.320 --> 00:09:01.000
GROWING PAINS:
One of the first struggles that DuckDuckGo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:06.920
ran into was funding. For obvious reasons, self&nbsp;
funding a popular search platform is rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:13.160
expensive, especially when you’re collecting no&nbsp;
data and showing fewer ads. For example, even with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:20.960
1.5 million searches per day, DuckDuckGo was only&nbsp;
pulling in $115,000 per year which was probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:27.120
not enough to even pay just their modest 3&nbsp;
employees. Fortunately though, in late 2011, Union&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:32.560
Square Ventures would agree to back DuckDuckGo&nbsp;
with $3 to $5 million which has gone a long way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:37.440
in helping them scale. But just as they finished&nbsp;
addressing their funding issue, they would run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:44.200
into another issue: Google. You see, it just so&nbsp;
happened that Google owned the domain duck.com.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:50.240
Google wasn’t even trying to spite DuckDuckGo.&nbsp;
Google had bought up a bunch of domains with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:56.320
simple words early on in case they ever wanted to&nbsp;
use them and duck.com just happened to be one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:02.600
them. Once they realized this though, they would&nbsp;
turn around use it to spite DuckDuckGo. They would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:09.160
basically just make duck.com redirect to Google&nbsp;
search. Honestly, from an outside perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:15.000
this was quite a funny troll move. But, I’m sure&nbsp;
it sure it felt a lot different from DuckDuckGo's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:21.320
perspective. Not only where they not able to lock&nbsp;
in duck.com, but duck.com was actively directing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:27.080
potential users to Google. Forutantely though,&nbsp;
Google would eventually come around. Whether this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:32.360
was truly out of good will or just a way to avoid&nbsp;
anti trust litigation, Google would hand over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:38.640
domain to DuckDuckGo in 2018 allowing them to&nbsp;
largely clear up the confusion. But even now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:44.760
DuckDuckGo is still not truly independent from big&nbsp;
tech. Ironically, their biggest challenge now is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:50.640
not Google but actually Microsoft. You see,&nbsp;
DuckDuckGo doesn’t actually run any of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:56.200
own searches. They’re more like a middle ware.&nbsp;
Whenever you search, they go ahead and mask your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.200 --> 00:11:00.840
identity and run the search through Bing. From&nbsp;
there, they’ll filter the results returned by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:06.400
Bing, remove trackers and targeted ads, and show&nbsp;
you the remaining results. For obvious reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:12.200
Microsoft isn’t exactly the biggest fan of this&nbsp;
solution leading to a constant back and forth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:18.920
battle between DuckDuckGo and Microsoft regarding&nbsp;
unmasking and remasking users. Despite all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:25.240
challenges, DuckDuckGo has managed to come out on&nbsp;
top in terms of popularity, though it is unlikely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:30.200
that they’ll ever be a true financial hit just&nbsp;
due to how their business is structure. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:35.320
they are profitable, they only pull in $100&nbsp;
million in annual revenue despite controlling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:42.800
2.5% of the search market. In other words, even&nbsp;
if they controlled 100% of the US search market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:49.520
they’d only be pulling in $4 billion. If we throw&nbsp;
in the rest of the globe, it might push that up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:56.800
$10 billion but that’s still nothing compared to&nbsp;
Google. 58% of Google’s revenue still comes from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.800 --> 00:12:03.480
search ads which adds up to $162 billion every&nbsp;
single year. So, DuckDuckGo won’t be living up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:09.960
to that anytime soon, but that was never their&nbsp;
goal anyway. Their goal was just to create a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:15.680
modest profitable private alternative to Google&nbsp;
and that’s exactly what they’ve done to great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:21.400
success. Would you consider using DuckDuckGo&nbsp;
over Google? Comment that down below. Another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:26.480
community oriented business that’s killing it is&nbsp;
Craiglist. Check out this video to learn more.

